{"name":"Pink Potion","hex":"#ceaebb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"},{"term":"potion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small portion or dose of a liquid which is medicinal, poisonous, or magical."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drug (someone)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/potion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Potion","blocks":[{"text":"A potion is a liquid that contains \"medicine, poison, or something that is supposed to have magic powers.\" It derives from the Latin word potio which refers to a drink or the act of drinking. The term philtre is also used, often specifically to describe a love potion, a potion that is believed to induce feelings of love or attraction in the one who drinks it.\nThroughout history, there have been several types of potions for a range of purposes. Reasons for taking potions have included curing an illness, securing immortality, and trying to inspire love. These potions, while often ineffective or poisonous, occasionally had some degree of medicinal benefits depending on what they sought to fix and the type and amount of ingredients used. Common ingredients in historical potions included Spanish fly, nightshade plants, cannabis, and opium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg/330px-Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potion","wikidata":"Q1249918"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Potions.png/500px-Potions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Potions.png/500px-Potions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A collection of vials labelled as potions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotions.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg/500px-A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg/1280px-A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg/500px-A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A quack or charlatan doctor selling potions from his caravan in 19th century Ireland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellco